{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"the-asherah-question","type":"text","text":"# The Asherah Question\n## Goddess or Symbol?\n\nBidirectional HIGH contradictions\nbetween three scholarly positions.\n\n**Not resolvable within current evidence.**\n\nThe -h suffix is genuinely ambiguous.\nArchaeological and textual evidence\ncan be read to support either conclusion.\n\n→ [[notes/synthesis/phase4-unknowns-and-convergence|Phase 4]]\n→ [[notes/synthesis/capstone-what-does-this-mean|Capstone]]","file":"/Users/littleseneca/Projects/Personal/OSKG-YahWeh/notes/synthesis/phase4-unknowns-and-convergence.md","x":700,"y":-3200,"width":420,"height":360,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"inscriptions","type":"text","text":"# Inscriptions\n\n## Kuntillet Ajrud\n\n\"Yahweh of Samaria\nand his asherah\"\n\n\"Yahweh of Teman\nand his asherah\"\n\n8th century BCE\nSinai caravan station\n\n## Khirbet el-Qom\n\n\"Yahweh and his asherah\"\n\n8th century BCE\nJudahite tomb\n\n## Key Point\n\nBoth inscriptions are\ngenuinely ambiguous.\nThe grammar can be\nread either way.\n\n→ [[sources/primary-sources/kuntillet-ajrud-inscriptions|Source]]","file":"/Users/littleseneca/Projects/Personal/OSKG-YahWeh/sources/primary-sources/kuntillet-ajrud-inscriptions.md","x":-1160,"y":2400,"width":380,"height":720,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"grammar-sub-dispute","type":"text","text":"# Grammar Sub-Dispute\n## \"His asherah\" — Same Evidence, Opposite Conclusions\n\nThe -h suffix on *asherah* is the crux of the debate.\n\n### Smith (MEDIUM confidence here)\n\n-h suffix = 3rd masculine singular possessive pronoun\n→ \"his asherah\" = \"his cult object\"\n→ Standard, expected grammatical reading\n→ Explains why a deity would not be \"possessed\"\n\n### Romer (HIGH confidence here)\n\n-h suffix = archaic feminine singular ending\n→ \"his asherah\" = \"his Asherah\" (the goddess)\n→ Grammatically possible, though less common\n→ Parallels personal name endings in Hebrew\n\n## The -h suffix is genuinely ambiguous.\n\nSame text supports both readings.\nResolution requires extra-grammatical evidence\nthat does not currently exist.","x":-2050,"y":-3620,"width":460,"height":700,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"bes-identification","type":"text","text":"# Bes Identification\n\n## Pithoi Drawings\n\nKuntillet Ajrud Pithos A\nfeatures two figures long\nidentified as \"Yahweh and\nhis Asherah.\"\n\n## Revised Identification\n\nRecent scholarship identifies\nthem as **Bes** — an Egyptian\napotropaic deity, not Yahweh.\n\n## Implication\n\nThe most famous visual evidence\nfor Yahweh-Asherah pair has been\nremoved from the table. Doesn't\ndisprove the goddess thesis, but\nremoves what was once considered\na pillar of the case.","x":-1350,"y":180,"width":380,"height":600,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"biblical-polemics","type":"text","text":"# Biblical Polemics\n\n## Royal Reforms\n\n- 1 Kgs 15:13\n  Maacah's Asherah removed\n- 1 Kgs 18:19\n  400 Asherah prophets\n- 2 Kgs 23:4-7\n  Josiah purges vessels\n\n## Prophetic Condemnation\n\n- Jer 7:18\n  cakes for Queen of Heaven\n- Jer 44:17-19\n  burning incense to her\n\n## Key Point\n\nPolemics prove practice\nexisted. But do they prove\na goddess or a misattributed\nsymbol?","x":840,"y":1920,"width":380,"height":680,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ugaritic-precedent","type":"text","text":"# Ugaritic Precedent\n\n## Athirat = Asherah\n\nEl's consort in Ugarit\nWell-attested goddess\n\"Creatress of the gods\"\n14th-13th c. BCE texts\n\n## Transfer Pattern\n\nYahweh absorbs El's traits:\n- Aged, wise deity\n- \"Father\" title\n- Divine council head\n- Compassionate character\n\n## Key Question\n\nIf Yahweh inherited El's\nprofile, did he also inherit\nhis consort? Pattern says\nyes. Proof is absent.","x":840,"y":480,"width":380,"height":680,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"figurines","type":"text","text":"# Figurines\n\n## 3,000+ JPFs\n\nJudean Pillar Figurines\nIron II (8th-6th c. BCE)\nFemale figure\nProminent breasts\nDomestic contexts\nHousehold religion\n\n## Cult Stands\n\nTaanach cult stand\nFemale figures\nDivine imagery\nRitual furniture\n\n## Key Question\n\nGoddess figurines\nor fertility talismans?\nSame objects, different\ninterpretive frameworks.","x":1030,"y":-420,"width":380,"height":650,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"iconographic-gap","type":"text","text":"# Iron IIB\n# Iconographic Gap\n\n**Keel & Uehlinger**\n*Gods, Goddesses, and Images\nof God in Ancient Israel*\n\n## The Problem\n\nJPFs appear suddenly in\nIron IIC (8th-6th c. BCE).\n\nThere is a gap in goddess\nimagery during Iron IIB\n(10th-9th c. BCE).\n\n## Implication\n\nJPFs may be late revival,\nnot continuous worship from\nBronze Age Canaanite religion.\nUndermines the \"Israel always\nhad a goddess\" narrative.","x":2320,"y":-810,"width":380,"height":650,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"dever-position","type":"text","text":"# Dever: Real Goddess\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Supports:** 3 edges\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 3,000+ Judean Pillar Figurines\n  in domestic contexts\n- Kuntillet Ajrud + Khirbet el-Qom\n  inscriptions naming Asherah\n- Biblical polemics prove she was\n  worshipped — you don't polemicize\n  against nothing\n- Archaeology shows widespread\n  folk religion with goddess figure\n\n## Stakes\n\nIf Dever is right → Yahweh had\na consort in popular religion.\nAsherah was real deity suppressed\nby later reform movements.","x":-240,"y":-810,"width":420,"height":610,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"smith-retreat","type":"text","text":"# Smith's Retreat\n\n## \"Genuinely Unresolved\"\n\nMark S. Smith, the strongest\nadvocate for the cult-symbol\nposition, has softened his\nstance over time.\n\n**Quote:** The question of\nwhether *asherah* refers to\na goddess or a cult symbol is\n\"genuinely unresolved\" given\nthe current state of evidence.\n\n## Significance\n\nThe scholar most committed to\nthe symbol position now concedes\nthe evidence is insufficient\nto decide. Stalemate acknowledged\nby both sides.","x":-3840,"y":-2080,"width":380,"height":380,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"romer-position","type":"text","text":"# Romer: El's Consort → Yahweh's\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Supports:** 6 edges\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Ugaritic precedent: Athirat\n  is El's consort, well-attested\n- As El's traits transferred to\n  Yahweh, so did his consort\n- Comparative ANE pattern:\n  consort transfer is normal\n- \"His asherah\" = goddess name\n  with archaic feminine -h\n\n## Stakes\n\nIf Romer is right → Asherah\ncame with El-Yahweh merger.\nGoddess worship was structural,\nnot merely popular practice.","x":4640,"y":-1430,"width":420,"height":620,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"smith-position","type":"text","text":"# Smith: Cult Symbol\n\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n**Supports:** 14 edges\n**Hinge:** #8 (9 dependents)\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Grammatical ambiguity of -h suffix:\n  possessive pronoun, not goddess\n- Biblical texts treat *asherah*\n  as wooden pole, planted object\n- Deuteronomy 16:21 — *asherah*\n  planted as tree beside altar\n- \"His asherah\" = cult object,\n  not divine consort\n\n## Stakes\n\nIf Smith is right → no goddess\nin official Yahwism. Asherah\nworship was popular deviation,\nnot orthodoxy.","x":-2240,"y":-1700,"width":420,"height":620,"color":"2"}
	],
